A codec turns CapTP messages into bytes and back. It does not decide what a
message means or whether a reference is valid; that authority stays in the
session. Changing codecs changes the wire format, not application behavior.

pancake ships two:

| Codec | Use it when | Tradeoff |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [Syrup](https://github.com/ocapn/syrup) | Always, unless a measured pancake-to-pancake workload says otherwise | Compact and interoperable; finding an arbitrary field requires a scan |
| [Cap'n Proto](https://capnproto.org/) | Both peers are pancake and large or partially read payloads benefit from fixed offsets | Constant-time field access; larger frames and no outside interoperability |

[One message, two encodings. Syrup lays it out as variable-length byte records and Cap'n Proto as fixed 8-byte words; decode either and you get back the same message.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-codec.svg'))

## Syrup

Syrup is the OCapN format and pancake's default. It is the only codec pancake
uses with [Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) and other
implementations.

Its length-prefixed, variable-width records keep small control messages
compact. The decoder can find a frame boundary and decode it in one forward
pass. Random access is the tradeoff: reaching item *i* requires walking the
records before it.

Syrup is also pancake's signing format. Session location signatures and
three-party handoff certificates use canonical Syrup bytes even when two
pancake peers exchange their messages with Cap'n Proto.

## Cap'n Proto

Cap'n Proto lays messages out in 8-byte words with fixed field offsets. That
makes partial reads and random access cheap, but padding and pointer words make
small messages larger.

In pancake it belongs to the `pancake-next` profile and is only for two pancake
peers. It never goes to Goblins. Select it with `codec = .capnp` in Zig
`SessionOptions` in Zig or the matching Host/binding option.

## Choosing

Stay on Syrup for ordinary CapTP traffic. It is the interoperability floor, the
signing format, and the smaller wire representation.

Consider Cap'n Proto only when both peers are pancake and your payloads are
large or field-heavy enough for fixed-offset access to matter. Measure your
actual message shape: the
[performance page]($link.page('performance')) shows one small mixed-argument
call, not a universal result.

Both codecs decode borrowed views and materialize owned values only when the
session must retain a message. [Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) explains how
peers negotiate the surrounding protocol profile. The
[architecture doc](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/docs/architecture.md)
defines the codec extension boundary for Zig consumers.
